      \field{abstract}{In this paper, we demonstrate that accelerated aging tests of power electronic components (specifically Si-based N-{MOSFETs} in our case) have an adverse effect on the conducted emissions (common and differential mode) in a 3-phase inverter used to drive an induction motor. To achieve this, we conduct electric accelerated aging tests while operating the power transistors to measure their conducted emissions before and after aging. A comparison between measurements in the time and frequency domains is presented to deduce the effect of aging on the Sibased N-{MOSFETs}. Experimental results show that after aging, there is an increase in the amplitude of conducted electromagnetic interference ({EMI}). Furthermore, this increase leads to electrical parameter degradation that has been investigated in {EMI} tests.}
